\f1\b \cf4 edge-disjoint.
\f0\b0 \cf2  All three models are closed, for every \cf5 n\cf2  and \cf5 m\cf2 . Simple graphs are Mader's\
  theorem, \cf5 \uc0\u8970 m(n-1)/2\u8971 \cf2 , which you cite. Multigraphs give \cf5 (m-1)(n-1)\cf2 . Hypergraphs give\
  \cf5 \uc0\u8970 (m-1)(n-1)/(r-1)\u8971 \cf2  via the hypergraph Gomory-Hu machinery. Nothing outstanding.\
\
  
\f1\b \cf4 Undirected,
\f0\b0 \cf2  
\f1\b \cf4 vertex-disjoint.
\f0\b0 \cf2  Simple graphs are Leonard through \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 \uc0\u8804 \cf2  \cf5 4\cf2  and S\'f8rensen-Thomassen at \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 =\cf2 \
  \cf5 5\cf2 , both cited, and open since 1974 from \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 \uc0\u8805 \cf2  \cf5 6\cf2 . Not your problem to move. The hypergraph row is your\
  own contribution: \cf5 thm:hyper-vertex-m2\cf2  and \cf5 thm:hyper-vertex-m3\cf2  settle \cf5 k_m^\{(r)\}(n)\cf2  for all \cf5 n\cf2  and all\
  \cf5 r\cf2 , showing it coincides with the edge value, with \cf5 lem:incidence-rank\cf2  doing the work at \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 =\cf2  \cf5 3\cf2  via the\
  Tutte/SPQR decomposition. Open at \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 \uc0\u8805 \cf2  \cf5 4\cf2 , where you'd need a 4-connectivity analogue that doesn't\
  exist. I checked eleven \cf5 (n,r)\cf2  cells at \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 =\cf2  \cf5 4\cf2  today and found no counterexample, but also proved the\
  surrounding formula has a genuine boundary: at \cf5 r\cf2  \cf5 =\cf2  \cf5 2\cf2  it 
\f2\i is
\f0\i0  the multigraph vertex problem, where it\
  provably fails from \cf5 m\cf2  \cf5 =\cf2  \cf5 5\cf2 . The two multigraph vertex rows aren't separate questions under your\
